{
  "_comment": "The default binding of @shexjs/neighborhood-sparql's manifest vocabulary, stacked onto ../../doc/webapp-manifest-context.jsonld's core (see doc/manifest-vocabulary.md for the survey this came out of). A manifest entry that names this neighborhood (neighborhood: sparql, a core attribute) writes this backend's own parameters under one `sparql:parms` mapping -- lexically scoped, like every extension's and neighborhood's. This file only binds names: the sparql: prefix and the sparql:parms term, whose property-scoped @context (JSON-LD 1.1) is the file beside it, manifest-parms-context.jsonld. That file defines the mapping's keys, so `endpoint` is written bare inside it and still expands to sparql:endpoint, and can never collide with the same-shaped attribute another backend defines, because outside its scope it is undefined. @protected: if another context stacked with this one defines sparql: or sparql:parms differently, a JSON-LD processor reports a protected term redefinition instead of silently letting the later one win; a manifest that needs both leaves this file out and binds a name of its own to the scope file (doc/tests-manifest-ld.yaml's header shows how). This is a tentative home: a SPARQL endpoint isn't specific to ShEx or to shex.js, so other RDF tooling may want this vocabulary too. If a better shared home turns up (a SPARQL-protocol-adjacent namespace, say), this prefix should point there instead.",

  "@context": {
    "@version": 1.1,
    "@protected": true,
    "sparql": "http://shex.io/neighborhoods/sparql#",

    "sparql:parms": {
      "@id": "sparql:parms",
      "@context": "manifest-parms-context.jsonld"
    }
  }
}
